.\" stylegen(1) -- man-macro reference page
.\" $Revision: 1.1 $
.\" $Log: stylegen.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/09/07 18:09:44  dlr
.\" Initial revision: stylegen(1), documenting 1.7.
.\"
.\" Thirty-six styles measured rather than assumed: 15 substitution, 6 combining, flip, 2 normalisation, 12 non-Latin. Per-script coverage given for all eight scripts tested, including Hebrew and Devanagari at 7 each, which the docstring does not state.
.\"
.\" The two inverse modes are the part that matters and the part easiest to confuse. --strip decomposes and drops every combining mark, so it unstyles, deaccents and de-Zalgos; --unstyle recomposes and keeps the marks. The hash covers bytes, so a grammar trained on a deaccented form can never generate the accented password it came from: --unstyle is the one to put in front of a trainer, --strip the one for Zalgo.
.\"
.\" Records two behaviours that are in neither the docstring nor the usage text: standard input is '-' only, the word stdin is opened as a file and dies, which is an inconsistency with rling and rehex; and exit status is 0, 1 for an unknown style or unopenable file, and 2 from argparse for an unrecognised option.
.\"
.\" Clean under mandoc -T lint and groff -man -ww with stderr empty; longest rendered line 78 columns; pure ASCII.
.\"
.TH STYLEGEN 1 "2026-09-07" "stylegen 1.7" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
stylegen \- generate and undo "fancy text generator" styled password variants
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stylegen
.B \-\-list
.br
.B stylegen
.RB [ \-\-styles
.IR style [, style ...]]
.RB [ \-\-hex ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-digits ]
.br
.in +8n
.RB [ \-\-keep\-identity ]
.RB [ \-\-label ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.in
.br
.B stylegen
.BR \-\-strip | \-\-unstyle
.RB [ \-\-hex ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stylegen
reads candidate words and writes styled variants of each, replicating what
the lingojam and fancytextguru class of websites produce: a whole-string
codepoint substitution, in which every character that the chosen style has a
glyph for is replaced and every character it does not have a glyph for
passes through unchanged.
.PP
That pass-through is the point.  The sans-serif bold italic block defines no
digits, so
.B Henrique20
styles to eight mathematical letters followed by an ASCII
.B 2
and
.BR 0 .
A real solved hash from that family:
.PP
.RS
.nf
b91cb86bee9ec2afe401fb3caf37807b1e011449:Henrique20
.fi
.RE
.PP
The digest is SHA-1 of the styled bytes; the base word the operator supplied
was the ASCII
.BR Henrique20 .
Its first character is <U+1D643>, MATHEMATICAL SANS-SERIF BOLD ITALIC
CAPITAL H.
.PP
None of these passwords were typed.  No keyboard layout emits <U+1D643>,
and macOS Unicode Hex Input takes four hex digits, so it cannot enter a
character outside the BMP at all.  They arrive by paste, which decides what
the base list should be: names, handles and display names \(em the sort of
string a person sets on a social media profile \(em rather than general
dictionary words.
.PP
Like
.BR procrule (1),
.B stylegen
never emits its source word; the caller supplies the base list either way.
.SH "STYLE DERIVATION"
The alphabets are derived from the Unicode database at run time, never
hand-typed.  Every styled letter NFKD-normalises back to its ASCII original,
so scanning the database for that recovers each mapping exactly.
.PP
This matters because the Mathematical Alphanumeric block has reserved holes
where the letter is borrowed from the BMP instead.  SCRIPT CAPITAL B is
<U+212C> and <U+1D49D> is unassigned; fraktur's C, H, I, R and Z are the five
characters named BLACK-LETTER CAPITAL in the Letterlike Symbols block, not
the codepoints their neighbours would predict.  Naive base-plus-offset
arithmetic emits a non-character at every one of those holes, and a candidate
containing one cannot match anything.
.PP
The non-Latin alphabets come from the same database by a different question:
not
.RI \(lq "does this decompose to ASCII" \(rq
but
.I why
it decomposes.  The compatibility decomposition tags <isolated>, <initial>,
<medial> and <final> are exactly the Arabic positional forms, <narrow> the
halfwidth katakana, <compat> the Hangul jamo, and <font> the Greek half of
the mathematical alphanumerics.
.SH STYLES
Thirty-six styles are available, in five groups.  Because they are derived at
run time, the exact set follows the Unicode database of the Python that runs
the tool;
.B \-\-list
is the authority, and prints a sample of each.
.TP
.B "15 substitution styles"
.BR bold ,
.BR bold_fraktur ,
.BR bold_italic ,
.BR bold_script ,
.BR circled ,
.BR double\-struck ,
.BR fraktur ,
.BR fullwidth ,
.BR italic ,
.BR monospace ,
.BR sans\-serif ,
.BR sans\-serif_bold ,
.BR sans\-serif_bold_italic ,
.B sans\-serif_italic
and
.BR script .
These come from the Mathematical Alphanumeric block, which is Latin and Greek
only.  Seven of them define digits \(em
.BR bold ,
.BR circled ,
.BR double\-struck ,
.BR fullwidth ,
.BR monospace ,
.B sans\-serif
and
.B sans\-serif_bold
\(em and in the other eight the digits stay ASCII.
.TP
.B "6 combining overlays"
.BR strikethrough ,
.BR underline ,
.BR overline ,
.BR slash ,
.B enclose_circle
and
.BR enclose_square .
A combining mark is inserted after every character, so these work on any
script.  The two enclosing marks are the only way to circle a word written in
a script that has no circled block of its own.
.TP
.B flip
Turned characters, then the string reversed.  There are no turned glyphs
outside ASCII, so on any other script this reverses and nothing more.
.TP
.BR nfc ", " nfd
Normalisation.  Same glyphs, different
.IR bytes ,
and the hash covers bytes.  Korean gains most: one precomposed syllable of
three bytes decomposes to three jamo and nine bytes.  Japanese and Vietnamese
move too.  A script with no composed form returns the input and is dropped as
identity.
.TP
.B "12 non-Latin alphabets"
.BR arabic_isolated ,
.BR arabic_initial ,
.BR arabic_medial ,
.BR arabic_final ,
.BR arabic_font ,
.BR greek_compat ,
.BR greek_font ,
.BR hangul_compat ,
.BR hangul_circle ,
.BR hangul_narrow ,
.B katakana_circle
and
.BR katakana_narrow .
The Hangul alphabets map jamo, but Korean text is written in precomposed
syllables, so those styles decompose the word first and then substitute.
.SH "IDENTITY SUPPRESSION"
A style that has no glyph for the script it is handed returns the word
unchanged.  All 15 substitution styles pass an Arabic, Cyrillic, Devanagari
or Hebrew word straight through, which made 75% of the output for a non-Latin
base word an exact copy of the input: measured on a 94%-non-Latin base, 800,000
output lines of which 237,500 were real.  A run built on that is
three-quarters a plain dictionary attack wearing a costume, and its hits are
all unstyled words that were already solved.
.PP
Identity output is therefore suppressed by default.
.B \-\-keep\-identity
restores it.
.PP
.B "CANARY WARNING."
If a run is gated by grepping the generated stream for a known plaintext,
that canary must be a word the styles can actually transform \(em Latin or
ASCII.  A non-Latin canary styles to itself, is dropped as identity, and the
gate then aborts a perfectly healthy run.
.SH "NON-LATIN SCRIPTS"
Styles that emit anything for one word of each script, under
.BR "\-\-styles all" :
.PP
.RS
.nf
ASCII / Latin      22
Arabic             12
Korean             10
Japanese           10
Chinese             7
Cyrillic            7
Hebrew              7
Devanagari          7
.fi
.RE
.PP
Chinese, Cyrillic, Hebrew and Devanagari have no substitution alphabet in
Unicode at all.  The 55 circled CJK ideographs are numerals and markers, not
letters, and Cyrillic offers three superscripts.  For those four scripts the
honest style set is the six combining overlays plus
.BR flip ;
.B nfc
and
.B nfd
return the input and are dropped.  Knowing that here is cheaper than
concluding it from a run that returned nothing but the words it was fed.
.SH "INVERSE MODES"
.B \-\-strip
and
.B \-\-unstyle
run the transformation backwards, recovering a base word from a styled or
decorated one.  They differ in one step, and the distinction is the point.
.PP
.B \-\-strip
compatibility-decomposes and then drops every combining mark.  It unstyles,
deaccents and de-Zalgos in a single pass.
.PP
.B \-\-unstyle
compatibility-decomposes and then recomposes with NFC.  It unstyles, and an
accent returns to the single codepoint it arrived as.
.PP
Use
.B \-\-unstyle
to normalise plaintexts before training a grammar with
.BR pcfg (1).
The hash covers bytes, and
.B Munoz
and
.B Mu
followed by n-with-tilde and
.B oz
are different passwords with different digests, so a grammar trained on the
deaccented form learns a terminal it can never turn back into the password it
came from.  Use
.B \-\-strip
for the Zalgo case, where dropping the marks is the whole point.
.PP
Zalgo-class decoration \(em randomised combining-mark injection \(em cannot be
generated at any useful scale.  Thirteen characters each carrying zero to
three marks drawn from a pool of eleven is 1.4e41 combinations, and the full
<U+0300> to <U+036F> block is 9.3e79.  It can be undone, and that is what
makes such a password useful: it is intelligence about a base word, not a
target.
.PP
Both inverse modes decode
.B $HEX[]
on input and honour
.BR \-\-hex .
.BR \-\-styles ,
.BR \-\-label ,
.B \-s
and
.B \-\-keep\-identity
do not apply to them.  If both are given,
.B \-\-unstyle
wins.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print every style with a sample and whether it defines digits, then exit.
Latin styles are sampled with
.BR Henrique20 ,
the non-Latin ones with a word of the script they serve.
.TP
.BI \-\-styles " list"
Comma-separated list of styles to apply.  The default,
.BR all ,
applies every style.  An unknown name is a fatal error naming the offender.
.TP
.B \-\-hex
Wrap any output line that is not pure ASCII in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]\fR,
the toolchain's interchange form; see
.BR rehex (1).
Almost all styled output is non-ASCII, so this wraps nearly every line.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-digits
Never style digits, even in the seven blocks that define them.  Affects only
the substitution styles; the overlays and the non-Latin alphabets are
unchanged.  Use it when the base list carries years or suffixes that the
victim is likely to have typed after pasting a styled name.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-identity
Also emit variants identical to the input word.  See
.BR "IDENTITY SUPPRESSION" .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stats
On exit, write per-style emitted and suppressed counts to standard error.
This is what makes a pointless arm of a run visible before it is run rather
than after: a style reporting zero emitted against the whole base list has
nothing to contribute to the attack.
.TP
.B \-\-label
Prefix each candidate with its style name and a TAB, so that a hit can be
attributed to a style without reconstructing the run.  The prefix makes the
line unusable as a candidate, so this is a diagnostic mode.
.TP
.B \-\-strip
Inverse mode: recover the base word.  Compatibility-decomposes and drops
every combining mark.  See
.BR "INVERSE MODES" .
.TP
.B \-\-unstyle
Inverse mode with marks preserved: compatibility-decompose, then recompose
with NFC.  This is the mode to feed a
.BR pcfg (1)
trainer.  New in 1.7.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the module documentation, which carries the usage summary, and exit 0.
.SH "INPUT AND OUTPUT"
Input is read as bytes and output written as bytes, never as locale-encoded
text.  Under
.B LC_ALL=C
a text write of a styled candidate raises an encoding error, and a run that
swallows it completes with an empty file that the attack afterwards reports
as a clean zero.  Reading bytes matters for the same reason: a locale-driven
decoder dies on the first accented byte of a UTF-8 wordlist.
.PP
A line of the form
.BI $HEX[ hexdigits ]
is decoded on input, as it is everywhere else in this toolchain.  The corpora
depend on that:
.B hashmob.net_*.found
and
.B hashpwn.txt
carry no raw high bytes at all, so every foreign word in them arrives
wrapped, and styling the wrapper instead of the word produces output that
looks plausible and is nonsense.  A malformed payload \(em non-hex, odd
length, unterminated \(em falls through and is styled literally, because that
is what it is.  The ASCII-payload case matters as much as the foreign one,
since many
.B $HEX[]
plaintexts are pure ASCII wrapped only because they contain a colon.
.PP
Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are carried through by surrogate escape and
re-emitted unchanged, byte-exact in plain mode and hex-wrapped under
.BR \-\-hex .
Passwords are bytes rather than text, which is the entire reason
.B $HEX[]
exists.
.PP
With no
.I file
argument
.B stylegen
reads standard input.  The name
.B \-
also means standard input; unlike
.BR rling (1)
and
.BR rehex (1),
the word
.B stdin
is not special and is opened as a file.  Empty lines are skipped and produce
no output.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion.  1 for an unknown style name, or for an input file
that cannot be opened, which is reported as a Python traceback.  2 for an
unrecognised command-line option.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the styles and see what each does to
.BR Henrique20 :
.PP
.RS
.nf
stylegen \-\-list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate every style of a handle list, wrapped for safe piping, and search a
hash file with it.
.BR mdxfind (1)
consumes the
.B $HEX[]
form natively:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stylegen \-\-hex handles.txt > styled.txt
mdxfind \-m e1,e8 \-f hashes.txt styled.txt > results.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reproduce the worked example above.  The digest is taken over the styled
bytes with the trailing newline removed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ echo Henrique20 | stylegen \-\-styles sans\-serif_bold_italic \e
      | tr \-d '\en' | openssl sha1
SHA1(stdin)= b91cb86bee9ec2afe401fb3caf37807b1e011449
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find out which styles are worth running against a base list before spending
the hash rate on them.  Output is discarded; the counts go to standard error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stylegen \-s base.txt > /dev/null
.fi
.RE
.PP
Attribute a hit to the style that produced it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stylegen \-\-label \-\-styles fraktur,script,circled base.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Normalise a set of recovered plaintexts, keeping accents, and train a grammar
on the result:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stylegen \-\-unstyle found.txt | rling stdin bases.txt
pcfg \-t bases.txt \-g grammar.d
.fi
.RE
.PP
Recover the base words from Zalgo-decorated plaintexts, where dropping the
combining marks is the object:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stylegen \-\-strip zalgo.txt | rling stdin words.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Style a non-Latin base list, taking only the styles that can do anything with
it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stylegen \-\-styles strikethrough,underline,overline,slash,\e
enclose_circle,enclose_square,flip,nfd \-\-hex cyrillic.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B stylegen
must keep running under Python 3.4.3, which is what
.B gp.bungi.com
carries, and that is the host the corpora are on.  The constraint is visible
in the source: the alphabet derivation tests
.B "ord(d) < 128"
rather than
.BR str.isascii() ,
which is 3.7 and later, and the output path calls
.B binascii.hexlify()
rather than
.BR bytes.hex() ,
which is 3.5 and later.  Before 1.6 the output path used
.BR bytes.hex() ,
and
.B \-\-hex
\(em the one flag that exists to make output safe for a password corpus \(em
failed on every non-ASCII candidate on the one host that holds the wordlists.
No third-party modules are used.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR pcfg (1),
.BR procrule (1),
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR rehex (1),
.BR rling (1)
